P = [
"Provo Canyon School was a residential treatment center for teenagers in Provo, Utah, that ran for 55 years and helped make Utah the center of the troubled teen industry.{wiki,apm26} Abuse there was documented almost from the start. In 1980 a federal judge barred the school from polygraphing boys, censoring their mail and isolating them, and between 2015 and 2020 state licensers opened 341 investigations at its campuses.{chj,milonas} Universal Health Services (UHS) owned it from 2000. Paris Hilton's 2020 documentary brought it national attention, and after a run of injuries to teenagers in 2026, Utah revoked the licenses of both its campuses that July. It closed in August 2026.{chj,kuer26,und0717,sltrib0727}",
"Robert H. Crist and Jack Williams opened Provo Canyon as a school for boys in 1971.{wiki,chj} Its start was contested. Utah County revoked its building permit in 1973, and a 1974 Utah Supreme Court ruling let it operate as a school.{wp} The r/troubledteens wiki says it was also shut down in 1974 over allegations of child abuse and neglect and reopened later that year.{wiki} D. Eugene Thorne co-owned it from 1978 to 1986.{wiki} Charter Medical Corporation bought it in 1986 through a subsidiary, Charter Provo School, Inc., and owned it for 14 years.{chj,wiki} In 2000 Charter Behavioral Health Systems, by then in bankruptcy, sold it to UHS, which ran it as UHS of Provo Canyon.{chj,herald21,heal3} A girls' program opened in Orem and moved to a new campus in Springville in 2005.{wiki} By 2026 the school ran a boys' campus in Provo and the girls' campus in Springville.{apm26}",
"Much of the industry grew out of Provo Canyon. Robert B. Lichfield started there as a dorm parent in 1977 and became director of residential living. He went on to create the World Wide Association of Specialty Programs and Schools (WWASP), whose founders and much of its staff came from the school.{wiki} Karr Farnsworth worked there with Lichfield before becoming WWASP's president.{wiki,fornits}",
"The first legal challenge came from two boys the school held for other states' juvenile courts. On September 21, 1978, Timothy Milonas Jr., sent by a Nevada court, and Kenneth Rice, sent by an Alaska court, filed a federal class action against the owners, Williams, Crist and later Thorne.{milonas} The court found that the school read and censored boys' mail, gave them polygraph tests, locked them in isolation rooms and used excessive force, including the \"hair dance,\" a grip on a boy's hair.{milonas} In 1980 a jury found for the owners on damages, but the judge permanently barred polygraphs and mail opening, and allowed isolation and physical restraint only to contain a violent boy or in immediate danger.{chj,milonas} On September 13, 1982, the Tenth Circuit upheld the injunction. It ruled that the state was so involved in the school that it was \"a joint participant,\" and that the practices violated the boys' due process and First Amendment rights.{milonas}",
"Complaints continued under Charter. In September 1990 the school suspended a staff member accused of having sex with a 15-year-old girl four times, and Provo police looked into claims involving other students.{deseret90} David Taylor, admitted from October 1990 to August 1991, sued in 1995 for fraud, medical negligence and false imprisonment. A jury found the school 25 percent at fault and it was held liable for $7,500. On December 9, 1998, the Fifth Circuit ruled that, unlike in the Milonas case, the school under Charter was not a state actor.{taylor} Paris Hilton was sent there at 17 in 1999 and stayed 11 months. She has said staff hit her, gave her unknown pills, watched her shower and put her in solitary confinement without clothes.{chj,ksat20} Former students who attended from 1989 into the 2010s have described strip searches, sedation and frequent restraints.{chj}",
"The accounts did not stop when UHS took over. The advocacy group HEAL has said a child who escaped in 2000 died. It has also published a parent's 2004 complaint that staff broke a 15-year-old's nose and then forced a Haldol injection on the child, who was kept most of the day in the seclusion unit called Observation. A third HEAL account, from a grandfather in March 2007, said staff denied a 16-year-old girl medical care until her pneumonia became acute respiratory failure. In December 2009 the school sued HEAL for libel over the police call records and complaints it had published, and HEAL called it a SLAPP suit, meant to silence criticism.{heal3}",
"In the 2010s the state began restricting the school's licenses. On May 20, 2012, a 17-year-old boy who had walked away from the Provo campus stole an SUV and, fleeing police at more than 100 mph, hit another car and killed a 65-year-old woman.{ksl12} Utah put the Provo campus on conditional status. It did the same to the Springville campus in 2013 after a girl was injured in a restraint, and to the Provo campus again in 2015 after staff injured students in restraints twice; Provo was also cited for a locked seclusion room.{chj} In December 2017 staff and students punched each other in an unofficial \"fight club.\" In May 2018 a former staff member, Misifoa Tautofi Seui Matagi, 21, was charged with felony child abuse for hitting a 16-year-old boy there.{sltrib18} During stays in 2018 and 2019, a 14-year-old girl from Oregon's foster care system was pinned down nearly 30 times and injected with sedatives 17 times, The Salt Lake Tribune reported.{herald21} From 2015 to 2020 state licensers ran 341 investigations at the school's four campuses and substantiated 27 violations. From January 2016 to June 2020 police answered 160 emergency calls at the Springville campus.{chj,kuer20}",
"In September 2020 Hilton described the abuse she suffered there in her YouTube documentary \"This Is Paris.\" With the survivor group Breaking Code Silence, she launched a petition to close the school, which passed 45,000 signatures within days.{kuer20} On October 9, 2020, she led several hundred survivors to the school's front gates. The school said it could not comment on anything before its owners bought it in 2000.{ksat20} On February 8, 2021, Hilton testified for Senate Bill 127, which allowed surprise inspections of youth treatment centers and required them to report restraints. The bill passed the legislature that March.{fox13_21,herald21,yahoo21} In August 2021 Aaron Ross, who was sent there at 13 in 2001, sued the school, UHS and three staff members in federal court in New York.{dm21,docket} He alleged that staff member Justin Uale forced him to perform oral sex about a dozen times under threat of starvation. He also said he was held for days or weeks in a bare \"observation room,\" given Seroquel, Depakote, Trileptal and Celexa without a diagnosis, and kept indoors for two years.{dm21} The case was moved to federal court in Utah in January 2022.{docket}",
"The violence continued. On March 27, 2023, a resident at the Springville campus punched a staff member, who later died after surgery; police had been called to the campus eight times between February and April.{sltrib23} On September 9, 2024, staff member Mulimauga Tuiloma punched a 15-year-old girl in the face and told police he had been using a restraint technique. He pleaded guilty in April 2025 to misdemeanor child abuse and was sentenced to probation.{ksl24,ksl25} State inspectors substantiated more injuries in restraints: a staff member struck a client on December 1, 2024, another injured a client's face on April 13, 2025, and in February 2026 a staff member punched a client, who needed stitches in the mouth. In January 2026 officials said a staff member struck a child being restrained, and staff told investigators they hesitated to put restraint details in writing \"since those documents are public.\"{apm26}",
"Then came the incident that led to the school's closing. On May 18, 2026, a 13-year-old boy at the Provo campus was knocked unconscious in a fight and suffered a broken jaw and a brain bleed. Staff drove him to the hospital instead of calling 911, delaying his treatment by about an hour.{kjzz_may,kjzz_june} On May 29 the state took emergency action and barred new admissions, and on June 18 it put 10 conditions on the license.{kjzz_may,kjzz_june,sltrib0602} The boy's mother sued on June 15, saying staff ignored his warnings that he was not safe, and a second family sued over medical malpractice.{sltrib0615,kutv0615} That June a staff member lay on a client's legs during a restraint and fractured the client's foot. Utah's Disability Law Center visited both campuses and reported that teens were not safe there, citing violence, self-harm, poor medical care and chronic understaffing.{sltrib0623}",
"On July 6, 2026, Utah's Department of Health and Human Services revoked the Springville campus license and ordered the girls' program closed by August 6. It cited unnecessary restraint and aggressive physical contact, neglected care, low staffing and late background checks, and barred the owners from reapplying for five years.{kuer26,apm26} On July 17 it revoked the Provo campus license and ordered all services there stopped by August 16, citing failures to protect clients from harm and the use of cruel and unnecessary practices.{und0717,nfts,deseret0717,ap0719,ksl0717} UHS appealed both orders on July 27. The appeal did not change the August 16 deadline, and the school closed.{und0717,sltrib0727} Hilton said the state had \"confirmed what survivors have known all along,\" and in August she urged Utah not to let the school reopen.{kuer26,sltrib0804}",
]
